course: technology
lesson: 42
title: Olympiad: machines at home and in the factory ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad lesson mixes the machines of the whole course, from the factory floor to the kitchen, the bike shed and the lift, into puzzles where you must think in two or three steps.
explain: Most puzzles hide a trap: a machine that seems to make cold really only moves heat, a magnet that pushes away a metal it cannot pick up, a child who is old enough but not tall enough.
explain: Picture a puzzle card at a science club: «A chain wheel has 40 teeth and the back cog 20. How far does the back wheel turn when you pedal once?» Count teeth, not guesses: 40 teeth pass, the cog turns twice, and so does the wheel.
explain: People talk big and small too: «I've told you a million times» means many times, and «it's nothing, it's just a bit warm» about a swelling battery is a real danger played down.
explain: Read every word, find the one fact that cracks the puzzle, and check the answer against what really happens.
fact: On a bike, the back wheel turns as many times for each turn of the pedals as the back cog's teeth fit into the chain wheel's teeth: a 40-tooth chain wheel with a 20-tooth back cog turns the back wheel twice for every pedal turn.
fact: A bigger back cog makes pedalling easier but slower, good for hills; a smaller back cog makes it harder but faster.
fact: A fridge, an air conditioner and a heat pump do not make cold or heat from nothing: they move heat from one place to another, and a fridge lets the heat out from the pipes at its back.
fact: In a sorting plant, magnets pull out steel, a spinning magnet pushes aluminium cans off the belt even though aluminium is not magnetic, and air jets with cameras pick out the plastics.
fact: Freezing stops most germs growing but does not kill them; water gets bigger when it freezes, which is why a full glass bottle can crack in the freezer.
fact: A circuit breaker in the fuse box switches a circuit off when too much current flows, and only an adult resets it; if it keeps tripping, an electrician checks it.
fact: Birds can sit on a power line because they touch only one wire and not the ground; a kite string touching a line can let electricity pass through a person to the ground.
fact: In the UK children use a child seat or booster until they are 12 years old or 135 cm tall, whichever comes first.
fact: A lift hangs on cables over a pulley with a counterweight, and safety brakes grip the rails if a cable fails; lifts are not airtight, so the air will not run out if one gets stuck.
fact: A kettle clicks off because a strip of two metals bends when steam warms it; a lift door, a factory press and a quality-check camera all notice things with light.
fact: A crumple zone at the front and back of a car is built to squash in a crash so the people inside are slowed more gently.
fact: A bicycle has 2 wheels, a tricycle 3, a car 4, and a usual train carriage 8.
exercise: A bike's chain wheel has 40 teeth and its back cog 20. How many times does the back wheel turn for 3 turns of the pedals? | 6 | 3 | 2 | 60
exercise: Odd one out: which of these does NOT work by moving heat from one place to another? | A toaster | A fridge | A heat pump | An air conditioner
exercise: A steel can, an aluminium can and a plastic bottle ride the sorting belt. Which one does the spinning magnet push off? | The aluminium can | The steel can, as it is the one a magnet holds | The plastic bottle | None, since aluminium is not magnetic
exercise: Which order is right for a bread factory? | mix, shape, rise, bake, cool, slice | mix, bake, rise, shape, slice, cool | shape, mix, bake, rise, cool, slice | mix, rise, slice, bake, shape, cool
exercise: A bird sits safely on a power line. Why is a kite string touching the same line still deadly? | Electricity can flow through the child to the ground | Kite strings are always made of thin metal wire | Birds have rubber feet that stop all electricity | The line is only live where the kite touches it
exercise: A full glass bottle of water is left in the freezer overnight. What happens? | It can crack, because water grows as it freezes | Nothing, because glass is far stronger than ice | It shrinks, because cold makes everything smaller | The water stays liquid because glass keeps it warm
exercise: Which claim about frozen chicken is true? | Freezing stops germs growing, but they wake up when it thaws | Freezing kills every germ, so thawed meat can sit out for days | Germs grow fastest in a freezer, so meat must be eaten at once | Frozen meat has no germs because they all froze solid and died
exercise: Mia is 10 and 140 cm tall. Does she still need a booster seat in the UK? | No, she is taller than 135 cm | Yes, she needs one until she turns 12 | Yes, she needs one until she reaches 150 cm | No, every child stops using one at 8
exercise: Count the wheels: a train of 3 carriages and 2 bikes. | 28 | 24 | 26 | 30
exercise: If a lift's cable ever snapped, what would stop the car falling? | Safety brakes gripping the rails | The counterweight on the other side, which would hold the car up | The lift doors locking shut | The air inside, which would cushion it
exercise: What makes a kettle click off by itself? | A strip of two metals bends when steam warms it | A timer inside the handle that counts exactly three minutes | A tiny microphone that hears the bubbling | The water level dropping below the line
exercise: Which of these does NOT notice things with light? | The kettle's switch | The lift door's curtain of beams | The press shop's light beam | The quality-check camera
exercise: You switch a bike from the smallest back cog to the biggest. What changes? | Pedalling gets easier but slower | Pedalling gets harder but faster | Nothing at all changes | The bike gets lighter
exercise: Two toy cars hit a wall: one front squashes a lot, the other is stiff and hardly dents. Which would keep people inside safer? | The one that squashes, as it slows them more gently | The stiff one, as it is stronger | Both are exactly as safe as each other | The stiff one, as nothing in it breaks
exercise: «I've told you a million times to clean the dryer filter!» How many times was it really? | Many times, but nowhere near a million | Exactly one million times | Never once | Only once, last week, and never before that day
exercise: The circuit breaker trips every time the heater goes on. What is right? | An adult resets it once, then an electrician checks it | Keep switching it back on yourself until it finally stays on | Tape the switch so it cannot trip | Ignore it, it's broken
test: ★★★ «bruv my bike's in the biggest back cog, gonna be well fast on the flat innit» What is right? | No, the biggest back cog is easiest but slowest, good for hills | Yes, the biggest cog always gives the top speed on the flat | It makes no difference which cog the chain is on | Yes, but only while you pedal backwards
test: ★★★ «it's roasting, leave the fridge door open, it'll cool the kitchen» Will it? | No, the heat it takes in comes out of its back into the same kitchen | Yes, a fridge makes cold air, so an open one will cool any room it stands in | Yes, but only if you open the freezer door as well | No, because fridges switch off when the door is open
test: ★★★ «my e-scooter battery's gone a bit puffy and warm, no biggie lol» What does it really mean? | A real danger: tell an adult and never press or pierce it | Nothing, puffy batteries just hold extra charge | Press it flat again so it fits back in | Pop it in the freezer for an hour to cool it right down so it is safe
test: ★★★ «we got 2 bikes and a trike — no wait, 2 trikes. how many wheels in the shed then» | 10 | 7 | 8 | 12
test: ★★★ «lights went off when I plugged in the toaster, Dad flicked a switch in the box and they came back. magic or what» What happened? | Too much current flowed, so a breaker switched the circuit off | The toaster used up all the house's electricity for the day | The light bulbs got scared of the toaster and turned off | The power station switched the whole town off
test: ★★★ «we're stuck in the lift, air's gonna run out, smash the doors!» What is right? | Press the alarm and wait calmly; lifts are not airtight | Smash the doors and climb out between floors | Jump up and down to get the lift moving again | Hold your breath as long as you can to save the air until help comes
test: ★★★ Eva is 11 and 128 cm; Leo is 8 and 136 cm. Who still needs a booster in the UK? | Only Eva | Only Leo | Both of them | Neither of them
test: ★★★ «yeast is just a powder that makes bread puff, like baking soda innit» What is right? | No, yeast is a tiny living fungus whose gas makes the bubbles | Yes, yeast is a kind of salt with no life in it at all | No, yeast is the flour that has been ground extra fine | Yes, it fizzes like a sweet when water touches it
test: ★★★ «the washer spun like a billion times, it literally walked across the floor» What really made it walk? | The clothes bunched on one side, so the drum wobbled | It really spun a billion times in one wash | Its concrete blocks fell out of the back | The water froze inside it
test: ★★★ «if microwaves can't get through the door holes, how come I can see the food?» What is the answer? | The holes are too small for microwaves but big enough for light | The door glass is the only part that lets anything through | Microwaves are see-through, so light and microwaves pass together | You can't really see it; the picture is a screen
test: ★★★ A worker says: «the belt's jammed, just reach in quick, it'll be fine.» What should happen first? | Stop the machine before anyone touches it | Reach in quickly while it is still running slowly | Pull the jam out with a scarf so hands stay clean | Kick the belt hard with your boot until it starts moving again
exam: A chain wheel bearing 40 teeth drives a rear cog bearing 20 teeth. Determine the number of revolutions of the rear wheel corresponding to 3 revolutions of the pedals. | Six | Three | Two | Sixty
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the operation of a domestic refrigerator. | It transfers heat from its interior and releases it from pipes at the rear | It manufactures cold air, which it stores within its walls | It destroys heat by means of a chemical reaction | It cools the room in which it is placed when its door is left open
exam: It is given that a child in the United Kingdom is 11 years of age and 128 cm in height. Ascertain whether a booster seat is required. | It is required, the child being under both 12 years and 135 cm | It is not required, the child being over 10 years of age | It is required solely on journeys of more than one hour | It is not required, boosters being intended for infants alone
exam: Identify the item which a spinning magnet in a sorting plant expels from the conveyor belt. | The aluminium can | The steel can | The plastic bottle | The paper carton
